1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,024
OPay priced its New York IPO. Then it walked away.

2
00:00:05,074 --> 00:00:10,942
Here's what happened, and what it says about who captures Nigeria's fintech wealth.

3
00:00:11,692 --> 00:00:18,712
In late October, OPay priced 16 million American depositary shares at $19 each.

4
00:00:18,762 --> 00:00:26,785
That was the bottom of its range: about $304 million, at a valuation near $4 billion.

5
00:00:26,835 --> 00:00:30,911
Then, before a single share traded, the deal was called off.

6
00:00:31,661 --> 00:00:36,547
OPay blamed a regulatory inquiry in a foreign jurisdiction.

7
00:00:36,597 --> 00:00:43,361
It did not name the regulator or the subject, and called that market immaterial to its core business.

8
00:00:43,411 --> 00:00:46,335
Anything beyond that is speculation.

9
00:00:47,085 --> 00:00:52,932
Remember: pricing is not closing. No shares settled, so the money was never raised.

10
00:00:52,982 --> 00:01:01,837
Standard Bank had agreed to buy up to $200 million of shares in a private placement. That depended on the IPO.

11
00:01:01,887 --> 00:01:06,965
It is now in limbo, along with a planned secondary listing in Lagos.

12
00:01:07,715 --> 00:01:11,557
The filing shows a fast-growing, profitable business.

13
00:01:11,607 --> 00:01:22,957
Revenue in the first half of 2026 was $467 million, up from $197 million a year earlier.

14
00:01:23,007 --> 00:01:26,550
Nearly 90% of it came from Nigeria.

15
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:29,755
But the real story is credit.

16
00:01:29,805 --> 00:01:36,739
Loan financing made up just over half of revenue. Payments brought in about 28%.

17
00:01:36,789 --> 00:01:44,641
Provisions for credit losses tripled, to $118 million: about half of lending revenue.

18
00:01:44,691 --> 00:01:50,709
So investors will price OPay partly as a lender, not just as a payments company.

19
00:01:51,459 --> 00:01:56,964
Now the bigger question. Nigerians generate most of OPay's revenue.

20
00:01:57,014 --> 00:02:03,927
Yet the company is incorporated in the Cayman Islands, headquartered in Singapore, and chose New York.

21
00:02:03,977 --> 00:02:10,869
Venture funds must return dollars. New York offers deep growth capital and liquidity in dollars.

22
00:02:10,919 --> 00:02:16,446
Lagos offers naira, thinner liquidity, and investors who favour dividends.

23
00:02:17,196 --> 00:02:21,144
The briefing argues the fix is plumbing, not mandates.

24
00:02:21,194 --> 00:02:30,305
A growth board on the Nigerian Exchange. A fungible dual-listing corridor. A small late-stage allocation from pension funds.

25
00:02:30,355 --> 00:02:37,503
And a pre-IPO sandbox that surfaces regulatory inquiries before pricing, not after.

26
00:02:38,253 --> 00:02:48,793
What to watch: the regulator's identity, any refiling, Standard Bank's placement, and second-half credit losses.

27
00:02:48,843 --> 00:02:52,151
This is analysis, not investment advice.

